    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was reunified as a socialist republic on 2 July 1976, a year after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> as capital.</li><li>Wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and failed collectivisation left it isolated and poor until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doi-moi">Doi Moi</a> reforms of 1986.</li><li>Under one-party rule it became a major exporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and electronics and a significant Asian middle power.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Within three years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doi-moi">Doi Moi</a> reforms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> turned from a country that had to import <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> into one of the world’s largest rice exporters.</p></aside>
    </section>

