    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh.jpg" alt="Ho Chi Minh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ho_Chi_Minh_-_1946_Portrait_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was born from the victory of the north in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>. On 30 April 1975 North Vietnamese tanks entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> and the government of the Republic of Vietnam surrendered. For a year the south was formally administered by the Republic of South Vietnam, the state of the Provisional Revolutionary Government, but real power lay with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam">Vietnam Workers’ Party</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, which decided on rapid reunification rather than a long transition. Elections for a single National Assembly were held across the country on 25 April 1976, and on 2 July 1976 the assembly proclaimed the Socialist Republic of Vietnam, with Hanoi as its capital, the flag and anthem of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam, and Saigon renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> City. In December 1976 the Fourth Party Congress renamed the ruling party the Communist Party of Vietnam and confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-duan">Le Duan</a> as general secretary, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pham-van-dong">Pham Van Dong</a> as prime minister and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ton-duc-thang">Ton Duc Thang</a> as president. The new state joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1977 and inherited a country of some 49 million people devastated by three decades of war, with damaged infrastructure, unexploded ordnance and land poisoned by herbicides such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agent-orange">Agent Orange</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city.jpg" alt="Ho Chi Minh City" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Ho Chi Minh City</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ho_Chi_Minh_City,_City_Hall,_2020-01_CN-03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Steffen Schmitz (more photos), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The leadership set out to build socialism in the south on the northern model. Hundreds of thousands of former officials and officers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> regime were sent to re-education camps, some for many years. Private trade was abolished in 1978, a measure that fell heavily on the ethnic Chinese Hoa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cholon">Cho Lon</a>, and farmers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a> were pressed into collectives. Production fell, food was rationed, and from 1978 hundreds of thousands of people fled by sea as boat people, many dying on the way. A botched currency and price reform in 1985 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> of several hundred per cent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixth-national-congress-of-the-communist-party-of-vietnam">Sixth Party Congress</a> in December 1986 adopted <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doi-moi">Doi Moi</a></em> (renovation): households regained control of farming, private business was legalised, prices were freed and foreign investment was invited. Within three years <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> turned from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> importer into one of the world’s largest rice exporters, and it later became the second-largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> exporter after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Textiles, footwear, seafood and, from the 2010s, electronics assembled by firms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsung">Samsung</a> drove growth. Poverty fell from well over half of the population around 1990 to a small minority by the 2010s, and the population passed 100 million in the early 2020s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, folk religion, ancestor worship and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> coexist under state supervision.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/johnson-south-reef-skirmish"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johnson-south-reef-skirmish.jpg" alt="Johnson South Reef skirmish" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/johnson-south-reef-skirmish">Johnson South Reef skirmish</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Union_Banks_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RobertJordan from the Vietnamese Wikipedia, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-army-of-vietnam">People’s Army of Vietnam</a> emerged from the war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as one of the largest and most experienced armies in the world, and the new state used it almost at once. After repeated attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> regime of Democratic Kampuchea on border villages, Vietnamese forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> on 25 December 1978, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> on 7 January 1979 and installed the People’s Republic of Kampuchea under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heng-samrin">Heng Samrin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, Democratic Kampuchea’s patron, retaliated in February 1979 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-vietnamese-war">Sino-Vietnamese War</a>, sending a large force across the northern border that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lang Son</a> and other provincial towns before withdrawing in March after heavy losses on both sides. Border fighting with China continued through the 1980s, notably around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vi-xuyen">Vi Xuyen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-giang-province">Ha Giang province</a>, and in 1988 Chinese ships killed 64 Vietnamese sailors in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johnson-south-reef-skirmish">Johnson South Reef skirmish</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spratly-islands">Spratly Islands</a>. The occupation of Cambodia, which tied down up to about 180,000 troops and brought international isolation, ended with the withdrawal completed in September 1989. The army, which had exceeded a million soldiers, was then cut sharply. Since the 2000s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> has modernised its navy and air force with Russian equipment, including six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilo-class-submarine">Kilo-class submarines</a>, to defend its claims in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/le-duan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/le-duan.jpg" alt="Lê Duẩn" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/le-duan">Lê Duẩn</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_duan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> is a one-party state led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam">Communist Party of Vietnam</a>, whose role is written into the constitution. Power is exercised collectively by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> and the Central Committee, chosen at party congresses held every five years, rather than by a single leader. The general secretary of the party is the most senior figure, alongside the state president, the prime minister and the chairman of the National Assembly. General secretaries have included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-duan">Le Duan</a> (to 1986), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-van-linh">Nguyen Van Linh</a> (1986–1991), the architect of the reform era, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/do-muoi">Do Muoi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-kha-phieu">Le Kha Phieu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nong-duc-manh">Nong Duc Manh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-phu-trong">Nguyen Phu Trong</a> (2011–2024), who led a sweeping anti-corruption campaign known as the ‘blazing furnace’ that forced out senior officials, including two state presidents; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/to-lam">To Lam</a> succeeded him in 2024. Constitutions were adopted in 1980, 1992 and 2013; the 1992 text recognised a multi-sector market economy under state management, which the party termed from 2001 a ‘socialist-oriented market economy’. The National Assembly, elected from candidates approved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnamese-fatherland-front">Vietnamese Fatherland Front</a>, has gained some role in scrutinising laws and ministers, but independent parties, media and trade unions are not permitted, and critics and bloggers are regularly imprisoned. Provinces enjoy considerable economic autonomy, which has encouraged both local initiative and corruption.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="Moscow" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_and_the_Red_Square.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Department of State, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> reshaped the politics of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Its occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> regime that had killed well over a million Cambodians, but it was condemned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/association-of-southeast-asian-nations">ASEAN</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the West, and the resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-indochina-war">Third Indochina War</a> kept the region divided until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-agreements">Paris Peace Agreements</a> on Cambodia of 1991. Its alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, sealed by a treaty of friendship in November 1978 and membership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comecon">Comecon</a>, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> use of the former American base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cam-ranh-bay">Cam Ranh Bay</a>. The exodus of the boat people produced one of the largest refugee crises of the late twentieth century and created Vietnamese communities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, Vietnam normalised relations with China in 1991 and with the United States in July 1995, the same month it joined ASEAN, and it entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-trade-organization">World Trade Organization</a> in 2007. Its later success made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doi-moi">Doi Moi</a> one of the best-known examples, with China’s reforms, of a communist state adopting market economics while keeping one-party rule. Vietnam has served twice on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a>, and its careful balance between China and the United States, raised to a comprehensive strategic partnership in 2023, makes it a significant middle power in Asia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/doi-moi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/doi-moi.jpg" alt="Đổi Mới" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/doi-moi">Đổi Mới</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khu_trung_t%C3%A2m_th%C3%A0nh_ph%E1%BB%91_H%E1%BB%93_Ch%C3%AD_Minh,_nh%C3%ACn_t%E1%BB%AB_ph%C3%ADa_qu%E1%BA%ADn_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tokeisan at Vietnamese Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> has not declined or ended; it was transformed from a war-damaged, isolated command economy into an outward-looking trading state while keeping its political system. The turning point came in the late 1980s, when the failure of collectivisation, the cost of the Cambodian occupation and the shrinking of Soviet aid forced the leadership to change course. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doi-moi">Doi Moi</a>, the withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1991 led Vietnam to seek new partners: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> lifted its trade embargo in 1994, and a bilateral trade agreement followed in 2000. Since then the country has signed many free-trade agreements and become a major destination for manufacturing investment, particularly as companies diversified supply chains away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Problems remain, including corruption, inequality, pollution, an ageing population and the vulnerability of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a> to rising sea levels and upstream dams. Tensions with China over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paracel-islands">Paracel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spratly-islands">Spratly Islands</a> periodically flare. Vietnam today is a lower-middle-income country of about 100 million people, governed by the same party that proclaimed the state in 1976, and among the most trade-dependent economies in the world.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> united north and south under communist rule in 1976, fought wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doi-moi">Doi Moi</a> reforms of 1986 became a leading example of a one-party state that embraced market economics and rapid export-led growth.</em></p>

